You can use the | operator to specify multiple types, like this:
let variable: string | number = "hello";
This tells the type system that variable can be either a string or a number, but in practice it will only ever be one or the other. You can also use the as operator to explicitly cast a value to a certain type, like this:
let variable: string | number = "hello" as string;
This tells the type system that variable is a string, even though it technically could be a number. The as operator is useful when you want to perform operations on the value that would not be allowed if it was not of the specified type. For example:
let variable: string | number = "hello" as number;
console.log(variable + 5); // prints "hello5" not "hello5.0" as it would have done if variable were a number directly.assistant

In summary, you can use the | operator to specify multiple possible types for a variable, and the as operator to explicitly cast a value to a certain type. The type system will then enforce these rules at compile-time, ensuring your code remains type-safe.иласяеристиЎыџNЎыџNилактиЎыџNıldığında

